description: Proposed: move the let-go pin from e9789b7d to dbccbf0f, upstream main with the linear duplicate-key check in the data reader (nooga/let-go#1106), 40 commits past the current pin.
 
 Why now: the reader fix removes the cost that kept P12.37's warm compile over its bound, so the snapshot's pair-vector stopgap comes out with the move. On the llvm-parity branch it is three commits, verified so far by P9.1, P12.37, P13.1, P12.31 and P1.0; the full row set is running at that head.
 
 - The driver now requires the test namespace it names (#'test/deftest). It was loaded as a side effect, and the new lg loads namespaces lazily, so without the require the driver fails to compile with "Can't resolve test/deftest". The require changes nothing at the current pin.
 - lw_rt's letgo-commit, env.sh's lg path, ir-pipeline.sh, lane5.sh, the size-boot scripts, the legmacs build default, the README, the spec and the review corpora's scripts move to dbccbf0f.
 - P9.1's gensym count at load moves from 1133 to 1161.
 - The records of the old pin (D217, STATUS.md, the benchmark docs, the byte guard's history comment) stay as written.
 
 What it touches on your side: P8.0's byte guard and the size-boot measurements are recorded at the old pin and will need a fresh capture at the new one; P4.3 and P7.4 already need the boot-time probe.
 
 Facts that close it: F1, the full row set at the llvm-parity head passes on the new pin; F2, the pin move lands on main by PR, with P9.1 at 1161.

## Comments
[2026-11-02T07:47:07Z] Matt Parrett: Yes to the move, with F1 before the PR opens rather than after, since the full row set is what covers the 40 commits the five rows above do not. Keeping the pair-vector stopgap removal in the same PR is right: the two are one change in the snapshot's size, and a byte guard across two PRs would show the swing twice.
 
 Our side after it lands on main: a fresh P8.0 capture and the size-boot measurements at the new pin, as their own PR, since the guard compares against the pin recorded in lw_rt and a capture folded into the pin PR would guard nothing. P9.1 at 1161 is the number to expect in that capture. The driver require is fine at either pin, so it can go in first on its own if the row run drags.
[2026-11-02T14:59:29Z] Norman Nunley, Jr: F1 is not met yet: a full run on the new pin stopped at 29 rows after eight GC-lane rows failed (P2.1, P2.3, P2.4, P2.6, P2.9, P2.11, P3.0, P3.2). The llvm rows sampled so far pass. Four causes, each fixed on the llvm-parity branch, not yet committed:
 
 - Class names as values. core.lg's vector? and reduce-kv now compare (type x) with let-go.lang.ArrayVector and friends. The GC lane compiled a class name as an extern var that is never set, so vector? was false for every vector. A class-name var now lowers to the type wasm.core/type-of interns for a value of that class; a class the runtime has no type for fails the compile.
 - Lazy namespaces. The new lg does not load test as a side effect, so the four native-side runners that name test/ (native-test-runner, intrinsics-native-runner, jank-native, jank-reader-check) now require it.
 - An upstream constfold finding. constfold counts :eq as commutative and moves a Const operand right. let-go's = is not symmetric for a vector and a list: (= #{(list 1)} #{[1]}) is true natively, the reverse false. The new IR build makes a set of literals one Const, so (= #{[1]} x) became (= x #{[1]}). The lane now keeps = in program order. The same swap should affect the Go lowering, so the fix belongs upstream too.
 - The reader. let-go now reads a lone + or - at end of input as the symbol; both runtimes' readers do the same.
 
 With these, every GC row that failed passes except P2.4. Its map-order programs sit at their 5 s CPU limit on both pins (4.9 to 5.2 s), so they get a 10 s grant in the pin PR.
 
 Separately, type-id gave PersistentVector the id 39 that PersistentTreeSet's kind also gives it, so on the GC lane a sorted set's type printed as PersistentVector and equaled a large vector's type, at either pin. It is fixed in its own small PR, with a case in corpus/wasm/review-shapes.lg.
[2026-11-02T15:49:23Z] Matt Parrett: The equality finding is nooga/let-go#810, the nested map case you filed: the set case reproduces on v1.13.0 and on main (hashes equal, `contains?` true from the vector side and false from the list side), and a comment there now carries it along with the constfold consequence. The upstream fix belongs in `ArrayVector.Equals`, not in constfold's commutative set, since `=` is symmetric by contract; keeping `=` in program order in the lane is the right stopgap until then.
 
 The class-name lowering and the lazy-namespace requires read as pin-independent fixes, so they can land ahead of the pin PR if that keeps it small. The 10 s grant for P2.4's map-order programs is fine with their times recorded in the PR; the type-id collision PR I will review at its head when it opens.
[2026-11-05T14:21:15Z] Norman Nunley, Jr: F1 is met: one full run of all 121 rows passed at the llvm-parity head on the new pin, in 2 h 55 min on a quiet machine. The set is the corpus rows less P1.GATE, P4.3, P7.4, P8.0 and P11.x.
 
 Four more fixes since my last comment, all reviewed:
 
 - llvm rebuilt every set and map literal as a hash-set or array-map call, to keep native's fresh allocation. The new ir.build folds a literal of constants into one Const that native shares, so the lane now leaves both to ir.build.
 - On llvm, a regex inside a constant collection got a constant global of its own. That global was initialized after the collection, so the collection held nil. The regex's stand-in is now built in the collection's own init.
 - Integer/parseInt now raises native's "For input string" message on both lanes.
 - Both lanes read a Const whose aux was [:const <type> <value>] as the value, for a typeinfer rewrite no let-go since ff1e6dac makes. A program's own [:const :int 0] now arrives as one Const and read as 0. let-go's lattice, run as a GC program, typed every constant :any, which broke P10.6 and P12.5. The aux is now the value; plain-data.lg covers the literal.
 
 The equality fix you placed in ArrayVector.Equals is nooga/let-go#1113 (Fixes #810). It also hashes a finite seq as an ordered collection, the third item of #1034.
 
 Earlier full runs failed rows whose programs all matched, on suite time caps and CPU grants under load. Each such row passed alone.
 
 Known gaps against native on both lanes, which no row covers: Long/parseLong has no twin, Integer/parseInt takes no radix, and NumberFormatException isn't a catch class.
 
 Next is F2: main's rows on the main pin branch, which carries the GC fixes and stacks on #91.
[2026-11-05T15:12:38Z] Matt Parrett: F1 met is enough to open the pin PR. I will review it at its head when it appears, with #91 under it. The Const aux change and the literal folding both read as the lane following ir.build rather than mirroring it, which is the right side of that seam.
 
 nooga/let-go#1113 has two P2s open from the 11-03 review, both reproduced at its head against the new pin: a one-argument repeat hangs the new ordered hash, and a queue compared with a vector is equal from the vector side only. Until it lands, the lane's program-order = stays the stopgap.
 
